From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <DCHEM88@hotmail.it>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 23:25:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] mb/lenovo/m720q_m920q: Report the platform to the EC as the
 OEM does

The EC firmware of the NCT6687D (Lenovo M1UCT17A on the M920x) loads
its profile, the temperature sources and the fan tables, for the
platform that the BIOS writes into EC 0xa7d-0xa7f in a configuration
phase, when 0xa7f has its boot flag set. The OEM firmware does it at
every boot (OemDxe), and the EC also reloads the sources then, locked
or not. Never told, the EC keeps the profile of its defaults: 02 43 00,
which is a 35 W processor.

Do as OemDxe does on the Tiny boards (IDs 0x30-0x35), at the end of
device init: 0xa7d is 0x42, or 0x52 with a card on the PEG slot and
0x16 with an NVIDIA one; 0xa7e has ICE Thermal Alert on, as the OEM
setup does by default, the ICE mode and the TDP class of the processor
(from MSR_PKG_POWER_SKU: over 65 W, up to 45, up to 65, up to 35 W);
0xa7f has the boot flag and the policy after a power loss, the
power_on_after_fail option that the PMC and the Super I/O apply. Not
when resuming from S3: the EC keeps its profile in S3, and the OEM
firmware does not write it then, in DXE.

Add the ICE Performance Mode of the OEM setup as the "ice_mode" option,
Hardware > Cooling: Better acoustics (the default), Better cooling and
Full speed.

TEST=On an M920x with an i3-8100, writing 42 42 07 this way at runtime
makes the EC load its sources and 65 W tables: under a 4-thread load
the fan follows the CPU, 834 to about 3000 rpm from 45 to 94 C, and
back to the floor after. Better cooling: about 1400 rpm at idle, 3700
at 94 C. Full speed: PWM 255, about 7500 rpm. Built lenovo/m920x.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
